/* The header for this test is at the BOTTOM of this file, below the script.
   It is there because a constant inside a leading comment can swallow the
   rest of the file in the engine - see RMSTEST_56 and the directory README.
   Nothing below the script can do any harm. */

<PLAYER_SETUP>
  random_placement

<LAND_GENERATION>
  base_terrain GRASS

  create_land
  {
    terrain_type GRASS3
    base_size 6
    number_of_tiles 600
    zone 2
    land_position 30 50
  }
  create_land
  {
    terrain_type GRASS2
    base_size 4
    number_of_tiles 300
    zone 3
    land_position 80 50
  }

<ELEVATION_GENERATION>


<TERRAIN_GENERATION>


<OBJECTS_GENERATION>


<CONNECTION_GENERATION>

  create_connect_all_lands
  {
    replace_terrain GRASS ROAD
    replace_terrain GRASS2 ROAD
    replace_terrain GRASS3 ROAD
    terrain_size GRASS 5 0
    terrain_size GRASS2 5 0
    terrain_size GRASS3 5 0
  }

/*
================================================================================
RMSTEST_76 - does the road's terrain_size disc paint over the player's own
land right up to the origin tile, or does the engine stop it at the land's
edge the way the pre-2026-09-17 nearest-edge model assumed?
Feeds: docs/known-issues.md BUG-029, preview-design.md Sec.6.5
(src/preview/generator/connections.ts - origin-to-origin pathing, landed
2026-09-17 to fix roads stopping one land radius short of every TC)
================================================================================

THE QUESTION
The 2026-09-17 fix moved connection endpoints from "nearest passable tile
on the land's edge" to "the land's own origin tile", on guide wording and
one in-game observation that a road visibly reaches the TC on Golden Hill.
That fix's OWN consequence has not itself been measured: if the path now
runs all the way to the origin, and terrain_size's disc is centred on every
tile the path crosses (including the tiles nearest the origin), the disc
should paint a circle of radius 5 around the origin too - INSIDE the
land's own body, not just on the open ground between lands. Nothing has
confirmed the disc actually reaches that far rather than being clipped at
the land's boundary by some mechanism this project has not modelled.

METHOD
One land (GRASS3, the "player" land, land_position 30 50 on a Normal map)
connected to a second, ordinary land far to its east. Both the background
GRASS and GRASS3 itself are declared replaceable, and terrain_size is
declared for all three terrains at a flat, deterministic radius 5 with
zero variance - no randomness to blur the geometric read. If the disc is
clipped at the land edge, ROAD should approach GRASS3's patch and stop at
its boundary. If it is not clipped, ROAD should appear WELL INSIDE where
GRASS3's own patch would otherwise be - a visible bite out of the land,
centred on its origin.

WHERE THE ORIGIN TILE ACTUALLY IS
land_position 30 50 on a Normal (200-tile) map, converted per Sec.4's
border-corrected percent-to-tile rule, lands close to tile (60, 100) -
confirm this against the scenario's own reported origin/patch centroid
before reading the radius-5 disc against it, since Sec.4's asymmetric
rounding on the high edges is a small but real correction.

PREDICTIONS
  INTRUDES (matches the origin-to-origin model as built) -> ROAD tiles
    appear within a ~5-tile radius of GRASS3's origin, i.e. GRASS3's own
    patch shows a visible notch or hole centred there, and GRASS3's total
    tile count comes in measurably below what land generation alone would
    produce (compare against the land's declared number_of_tiles plus the
    usual small growth overshoot).
  STOPS AT THE EDGE -> ROAD approaches GRASS3's patch and the two bounding
    boxes touch or come close, but no ROAD tile sits more than a tile or
    so inside where GRASS3's boundary was before the connection ran.

GENERATE AT NORMAL (200), ANY PLAYER COUNT. TWO RUNS.

READ WITH
  python probe_scenario.py "<scenario>" --patches GRASS3
  python probe_scenario.py "<scenario>" --patches ROAD
  Read GRASS3's own patch centroid as the origin proxy, then check every
  ROAD patch's bounding box and nearest tile against it. A ROAD tile
  within ~5 tiles of that centroid, sitting inside GRASS3's own former
  footprint, is the intrusion reading.
================================================================================
*/
